course: everyday
lesson: 509
title: The favourite, the black sheep, covering for each other and hand-me-downs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Derby, Mum and Dad run a chippy, and their three girls have labels: Holly is «the clever one», Pixie is «the baby». Then Fern hears Nan say, «Fern's the one who's always in trouble.» Fern folds her arms: «I'm the black sheep then, am I?» A black sheep is the one who doesn't fit the family's picture. Labels stick, and they can be unfair, but a label isn't you. In Leeds the same happens to Tobias, who likes painting, not footy: his sporty brothers call him «the odd one», and his art teacher calls him «the talented one». Same boy, different eyes.
explain: Kids often feel there's a favourite. In Atlanta, Marquis gets a car for his 17th birthday; Ezekiel got a cheap bike for his 10th. «Bruh, the favoritism is crazy,» he tells his cousin. Sometimes it's real, and sometimes it's because different ages get different things. In London, Grandma gives Nkechi's brother the biggest piece of meat «because he's a boy». «That's not fair,» says Nkechi, calmly. Treating girls and boys differently like that is unfair, and many families are changing it, sometimes because a kid spoke up.
explain: Covering for each other can be funny. In Llandudno, Rhiannon breaks Mam's favourite mug. Big sister Cari jumps in: «Twas the cat, Mam» («'twas» is old-fashioned for it was). Mam: «We haven't got a cat.» Both girls burst out laughing and own up. In Galway, Darragh sneaks a biscuit and Aoibheann says nothing; next day she sneaks one and he says nothing. «Deal's a deal.» Small secrets like that are a sibling deal; anything about someone's safety is different and must be told. In Ohio, Mason-Tyler breaks the TV remote and his big brother takes the blame, but Mason-Tyler feels so bad that he confesses.
explain: Hand-me-downs are clothes passed down from an older child. In Croydon, Ollie-Ray's school trousers were his brother's and are a bit long. «Them trousers are bare long, bruv» («bare» is London slang for very), says his mate. Ollie-Ray grins: «Room to grow, innit.» Laugh first and the joke loses its sting. In Melbourne a girl says, «Isn't that your sister's dress?» Coco twirls: «Yeah, it's vintage, babe.» Everyone laughs, with her.
explain: Siblings change. In Edinburgh, Hamish and Isla-Mae hardly spoke, until their dad got ill last year; Isla-Mae started making Hamish toast and helping with homework. «She's no' sae bad now» (Scots: not so bad). Hard times can bring siblings closer. In Sheffield it goes the other way: Becca's sister turns 13, calls Becca «so cringe» and stops letting her in her room. That often passes; meanwhile Becca can give her space and find her own friends.
fact: Families often give children labels like «the clever one» or «the baby»; labels stick and can become unfair.
fact: A «black sheep» is the family member who doesn't fit the family's idea of itself; being different is not being wrong.
fact: Children often feel a parent has a favourite; sometimes it is real, sometimes different ages simply get different things.
fact: Telling a parent calmly, «Sometimes I feel you like him more», is a good way to bring it up; most parents want to know.
fact: Treating a child worse because she is a girl, or he is a boy, is unfair, and many families are changing it.
fact: Covering for a sibling is a kind of loyalty, but lies usually come out.
fact: Small secrets, like a sneaked biscuit, can be a sibling deal; anything about safety must be told to a grown-up.
fact: Letting someone take your blame feels bad, and owning up usually feels better.
fact: Hand-me-downs save money and are completely normal; laughing first takes the sting out of jokes.
fact: Hard times, like a parent being ill, can bring siblings closer.
fact: When a sibling becomes a teenager they may want space for a while; it often passes.
fact: «'Twas» means it was; «bare» is London slang for very; «no' sae bad» is Scots for not so bad; «bruh» is US slang for bro; «nick» means steal.
exercise: What did Nan call Fern? | «The one who's always in trouble» | «The clever one» | «The baby of the family» | «The quiet one who helps»
exercise: What is a «black sheep» in a family? | The one who doesn't fit the family's picture | The one with the darkest hair | The eldest | The one who works in the family chippy
exercise: Tobias's art teacher calls him «the talented one». What does his family call him? | «The odd one» | «The sporty one» | «The baby» | «The clever one»
exercise: Ezekiel thinks Marquis is the favourite. What is one other possible reason Marquis got more? | He's older, and ages get different things | Marquis paid for the car out of his own money | Ezekiel never asked for any presents | Their mom forgot Ezekiel's birthday that year
exercise: What's a good way for Ezekiel to bring it up? | Calmly: «Sometimes I feel you like him more» | Shouting at dinner: «You love him more than me!» | Sulking for a month until someone notices | Scratching the new car so it's not so special
exercise: Why did Grandma give Nkechi's brother the biggest piece of meat? | Because he's a boy | Because he's the hungriest | Because it was his birthday | Because he cooked it
exercise: Is Nkechi right that it's not fair? | Yes | No, boys should always get more | No, grandmas are always right | Only if her brother agrees
exercise: Cari said «Twas the cat, Mam». Why did they burst out laughing? | They haven't got a cat | The cat was asleep on the mug | Mam believed every word | The cat started to cry
exercise: What does «'twas» mean? | It was | It wasn't | Two of them | Then
exercise: What deal do Darragh and Aoibheann have? | Neither tells on the other about biscuits | They share every biscuit fifty-fifty | They tell Mam everything the other does | They take it in turns to do the washing-up
exercise: Which secret should a sibling NOT keep? | One about someone's safety | One about a sneaked biscuit | One about a surprise party | One about a birthday present
exercise: What did Mason-Tyler do after his brother took the blame? | Felt bad and confessed | Thanked him and kept quiet for ever | Broke another remote to make it fair | Blamed the dog for both of them
exercise: What does «bare long» mean about Ollie-Ray's trousers? | Very long | Barely long enough | Too short | Old and ripped
exercise: How did Coco answer the girl who spotted her sister's dress? | «Yeah, it's vintage, babe» | She ran off crying to the toilets | «No it's not, I bought it myself» | She told the girl her dress was ugly
exercise: What brought Hamish and Isla-Mae closer? | Their dad being ill | A holiday abroad | A new puppy | Sharing a bedroom
exercise: Becca's sister turned 13 and calls her «so cringe». What's wise for Becca? | Give her space, find her own friends, stay kind | Read her diary to find out what's wrong | Follow her everywhere until she gives in | Tell everyone at school her sister's secrets
test: «Bruh, Marquis got a CAR and I got a bike, Mom loves him more, facts». What's truer? | Maybe, but older kids often get bigger presents | Definitely, a bigger present always means more love | No, Mom secretly loves Ezekiel more | It's because bikes are better than cars
test: Holly is 12, Fern 9, Pixie 6. Holly's winter coat fits a 12-year-old and gets passed down. How many years until Pixie is 12 and wears it? | 6 years | 3 years | 9 years | 12 years
test: Mam has 6 mugs. One is broken, and Cari says, «the cat did it». The family has no cat. What does Mam know for certain? | Cari's story isn't true | Cari broke the mug herself | A neighbour's cat came in | The mug broke on its own
test: Fern, sulking: «Nan says I'm trouble, so I might as well be trouble, innit». What's a better way to think? | A label isn't her; she can show them more of herself | She should be twice as naughty to prove Nan right | Labels are always true, so she should give up | She should never speak to Nan again
test: «Grandma says boys need more meat cos they're growing, girls don't». What's right? | Girls are growing too; it's unfair | Only boys grow at Nkechi's age | Grandma is right, girls eat less | Meat is bad for girls
test: Aoibheann sees Darragh climb onto the shed roof to get a ball. «Deal's a deal, don't tell Mam». What should she do? | Tell Mam, it's about safety | Keep the deal, he'd keep hers | Climb up after him | Film it for a laugh
test: Mason-Tyler's brother keeps taking the blame for him. What would likely happen if it carried on? | Mason-Tyler would keep feeling bad | Mason-Tyler would feel great forever | Their parents would always know the truth | The brother would get a prize
test: A lad laughs: «your trousers are bare long, bruv, did ya nick 'em off your dad?» What's the best reply? | A grin: «Room to grow, innit» | Cry and go home | Punch him in the arm | Never wear trousers again
test: In a film, the family's «weird kid» is laughed at until the end. What's truer in real life? | Being different isn't being wrong | The weird kid should change to fit in | Families never label children | Different kids have no talents
test: Hamish: «Isla-Mae's no' sae bad now, she makes me toast». What does that tell you? | Hard times brought them closer | She is being paid to do it | She has always been nice to him | He now hates toast
test: Becca: «she says I'm so cringe and slams her door, she hates me now». What's most likely? | It's a teenage phase that often passes | Her sister will hate her forever | Becca did something terrible | Becca should slam her door back louder
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the labels customarily assigned to children within a family? | They tend to persist and may prove unjust | They are invariably accurate assessments | They are wholly harmless in every case | They are assigned only by teachers
exam: Identify the most appropriate means by which a child may raise a perceived parental preference for a sibling. | A calm statement of the child's feelings to the parent | A protracted period of sulking without explanation | Damage to the property of the favoured sibling | Complaint made exclusively to neighbours and friends
exam: Ascertain which category of secret ought not to be retained by one sibling on behalf of another. | One concerning safety | One concerning the consumption of a single biscuit | One concerning a forthcoming birthday celebration | One concerning a present purchased for a parent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the wearing of clothing formerly belonging to an elder sibling. | It is normal and economical | It is a source of lasting disgrace to the wearer | It is prohibited by the regulations of most schools | It indicates that the wearer is not loved
